prompt:
Use Three.js to implement a navigable 3D living room scene with procedural furniture and collision detection, output as a single-file HTML document.

[Three.js Import Method (Must Be Followed Strictly)]
Include the following importmap in the page, and use <script type="module"> to import three and addons as ES Modules:
<script type="importmap">
{ "imports": {
    "three": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/build/three.module.js",
    "three/addons/": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/examples/jsm/"
} }
</script>

[Room]
- Interior dimensions: 8m × 6m × 3m (recommended: floor at y=0, ceiling at y=3, and horizontal ranges x∈[0,8], z∈[0,6], or an equivalent translation, but remain consistent between the prompt and validation);
- The floor, four walls, and ceiling must be independent meshes with distinct materials (such as wood grain for the floor, painted material for the walls, and a matte material for the ceiling).

[Furniture (All Must Be Procedurally Assembled Using BoxGeometry/PlaneGeometry/CylinderGeometry, Etc.; Must Include at Least)]
- Sofa: assembled from multiple Boxes;
- Coffee table;
- Television: mounted against a wall, with the screen implemented using PlaneGeometry + emissive;
- Bookshelf;
- Floor lamp (clickable to toggle its light source);
- Two chairs;
- Rug: a flattened CylinderGeometry (radiusTop=radiusBottom, very small height) placed against the floor.
The total number of furniture items is included in furnitureCount (7–8 recommended).

[Lighting]
- AmbientLight for base ambient illumination;
- 1 ceiling PointLight to simulate a ceiling-mounted light;
- Use a SpotLight for the floor lamp (its intensity may change according to the switch state);
- Reasonable shadows are optional.

[First-Person View and Controls]
- PointerLockControls (three/addons/controls/PointerLockControls.js) + WASD movement and mouse look;
- Simple room-axis-aligned AABB collision detection: the camera/person collider must not pass through walls or enter furniture bounding boxes (each frame, clamp the position back into the valid area or push it back with sliding behavior).

[Click Interaction (Ray Picking)]
- Click the floor lamp mesh: toggle lampOn, changing the SpotLight intensity between "on/off" states;
- Click the television screen area: toggle tvOn; when off, the screen is nearly black, and when on, it has a blue emissive glow.

[Automated Testing Hooks]
- Attach the first-person PerspectiveCamera reference to window.__fpCamera__, and on every frame update __3D_STATE__.cameraPos from the actual camera according to its final position after collision resolution.

[HUD]
- id="camPos": camera position text, such as "x, y, z";
- id="furnitureCount": number of furniture items;
- id="lightsOn": the current number of light sources "counted as on" (for example, the always-on ceiling light counts as 1, and the floor lamp adds +1 when on; this must remain consistent with the implementation and be updated when lampOn/tvOn changes).

[State Exposure]
window.__3D_STATE__ = {
  cameraPos: { x, y, z },
  furnitureCount: number,
  furnitureList: [{ name, x, y, z }, ...],
  lightsOn: number,
  lampOn: boolean,
  tvOn: boolean
}

Output a single HTML file.
